  0. Definitions.

  "Fletcher" is the software the Fletcher Authors publish at
<https://github.com/eivacom/Fletcher>.

  A "Managed Library" is any of the .NET assemblies Fletcher,
Fletcher.Interop, Fletcher.Model and Fletcher.GatewayClient, built from
the sources under dotnet/src/ in Fletcher and published in the NuGet
packages Eiva.Fletcher, Eiva.Fletcher.Interop, Eiva.Fletcher.Model and
Eiva.Fletcher.GatewayClient. A Managed Library does not include any
native library those packages carry.

  The "Options File" is the file protoc/include/fletcher/options.proto
in Fletcher, which the package Eiva.Fletcher.Protoc also carries.

  The "Generator" is protoc-gen-fletcher, the protoc plugin built from
the sources under protoc/ in Fletcher.

  A "Released Version" of a Managed Library or of the Options File is
that file exactly as the Fletcher Authors released it, or as built
without modification from the Corresponding Source of such a release.

  An "Application" is any work that is not itself a Managed Library or
a modified version of one.

  A "Compilation Process" is any process that combines one or more
Managed Libraries with an Application into one or more files, including
ahead-of-time compilation (for example .NET Native AOT), single-file
bundling, trimming of unused code, and the merging or linking of the
resulting code.

  1. Compiling a Managed Library into an Application.

  You may convey a work that a Compilation Process produces from an
Application and Released Versions of one or more Managed Libraries under
terms of your choice, consistent with the licensing of the Application,
without the obligations of sections 4(d) and 4(e) of the LGPL for those
Managed Libraries, provided that you comply with sections 4(a), 4(b) and
4(c) of the LGPL. The removal of unused code by a Compilation Process
does not make a Managed Library a modified version.

  This permission does not extend to a Managed Library you have modified,
to which the LGPL applies in full, nor to the native libraries Fletcher
publishes with the Managed Libraries, which remain under the LGPL alone.

  2. Code generated from the Options File.

  Source code that a Protocol Buffers compiler (protoc, any of its
plugins, or a build tool that runs them) generates from a Released
Version of the Options File, and object code compiled from that source
code, may be used, modified and conveyed under terms of your choice.

  3. Output of the Generator.

  The files the Generator writes, including any part of them copied
from the Generator's own source, may be used, modified and conveyed under
terms of your choice. This permission does not extend to the libraries
those files use when they run: for C#, the Managed Libraries, under
section 1; for every other language, the libraries of Fletcher under the
LGPL alone.

  4. Removing this permission.

  As section 7 of the GPL allows, when you convey a copy of Fletcher or
of a part of it, you may remove this additional permission from that copy.

  5. Nothing else changes.

  Except as stated here, the LGPL and the GPL apply unchanged.
